            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest Perseverance rover news is that NASA reports the rover is investigating very ancient rocks on the rim of Jezero Crater that appear to record repeated asteroid impacts from roughly 4 billion years ago. The rover examined a 245-foot / 75-meter-thick stack of layered bedrock called the “Broom Point member,” likely more than 3.9 billion years old, making it among the oldest terrain ever studied by a Mars rover【6:1†source】.\n\nKey updates:\n\n- **Ancient impact record found:** Perseverance’s instruments detected breccias, pulverized rock layers, and glassy beads that point to repeated asteroid impacts rather than volcanic activity as the main origin of the Broom Point rocks【6:1†source】.\n- **Why it matters:** Because Mars lacks Earth-style plate tectonics, these ancient rocks may preserve geological history that has largely been erased on Earth, offering a rare look at the early solar system【6:1†source】.\n- **Major driving milestone:** Perseverance recently completed the equivalent of a full marathon on Mars — **26.2 miles / 42.195 km** — reaching that distance on sol 1,890 after about five years and four months of driving【6:0†source】.\n- **Faster than Opportunity:** NASA’s earlier Opportunity rover took 11 years and two months to cover the same distance, so Perseverance reached the milestone in less than half the time【6:0†source】.\n- **Orbital image:** NASA’s Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter captured Perseverance from above with its HiRISE camera shortly before the marathon milestone, showing the rover and its tracks west of Jezero Crater near a region nicknamed “Arbot”【6:0†source】.\n\nIn short: Perseverance is still healthy, mobile, and scientifically productive, now exploring terrain beyond Jezero Crater that may preserve some of Mars’ oldest geological history.",
                "annotations": [
                  {
                    "type": "url_citation",
